## Dependency compatibility (React/Next)
- Before adding or keeping any dependency, verify its **peerDependencies** are compatible with the repo’s current `react`, `react-dom`, and `next` versions.
- If a dependency is not imported/used in the codebase, remove it to avoid install-time failures (especially strict peer-dep conflicts on CI/Vercel).
- Always run `npm install` locally after dependency changes and ensure it succeeds without `--force` / `--legacy-peer-deps`.

## Vercel deploy security gate (Next.js)
- Keep `next` and `eslint-config-next` on a **patched** version; Vercel may block production deploys for known vulnerable releases.
- When upgrading `next`, upgrade `eslint-config-next` to the same version to avoid tooling mismatches.

## Vercel + Git (GitHub) connection
- `vercel deploy` may **still succeed** as an upload-based deployment even when `vercel git connect` fails. That does **not** mean the project is Git-linked.
- If `vercel git connect <repo-url>` returns *Failed to connect … private / access*, fix **GitHub → Settings → Applications → Vercel** (org/repo access) or use the Vercel dashboard **Project → Settings → Git → Connect Repository** and authorize the `adstryker` org (or whichever org owns the repo).
- Do not tell the user “deployed from Git” until the Vercel project shows a connected Git repository (no “Connect Git Repository” prompt on the project card).

## Vercel / Next.js `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` env vars
- Values are **baked in at build time**. After adding or changing them in Vercel, trigger a **new Production deployment** (Redeploy); old bundles will not pick up changes.
- Paste values **without** surrounding quotes; avoid trailing spaces/newlines. Prefer `normalizePublicEnvValue`-style handling in `lib/publicEnv.ts` when touching API base URL / app key.
- `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` must be a full absolute URL (e.g. `https://api.example.com`) — relative paths break axios `baseURL` during SSR/build.
